<ArticleLayout
  title="What Size Air Conditioner Do I Need? A Practical Australian Guide"
  seoTitle="What Size Air Conditioner Do I Need? Australian Guide"
  description="Measure room area, ceilings, glazing, sun, insulation and connected spaces, then get professional sizing and installation advice before buying a fixed system."
  category="Living Spaces"
  date="Published August 2026"
  datePublished="2026-08-19"
  readingTime="6 minute read"
  showAffiliateDisclosure={false}
  image="/images/what-size-air-conditioner-do-i-need.webp"
  imageAlt="An air-conditioning installer measuring a sunlit open-plan Australian living and dining area before recommending a system"
  socialImage="/images/what-size-air-conditioner-do-i-need.webp"
  socialImageAlt="An air-conditioning installer measuring a sunlit open-plan Australian living and dining area before recommending a system"
>
  <p>Choosing the right air conditioner is not as simple as matching floor area to a kilowatt figure.</p>
  <p>Room size is a useful starting point, but the cooling load can also change with ceiling height, insulation, glazing, orientation, afternoon sun, local climate and whether the room opens into adjoining spaces.</p>
  <p>If you are considering a permanently installed system, get installation quotes before buying where practical. A competent installer can assess the room, recommend an appropriate capacity and confirm whether the proposed installation is practical.</p>
  <p>If you are still comparing system types, start with the <a href="/guides/air-conditioning-buying-guide/">Air Conditioning Buying Guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Start by measuring the space</h2>
  <p>Measure the length and width of the area you want to cool:</p>
  <p><strong>Length × width = floor area in square metres</strong></p>
  <p>For example:</p>
  <p><strong>4 m × 5 m = 20 m²</strong></p>
  <p>Also note the ceiling height. A 20 m² room with a standard ceiling is a different cooling problem from a 20 m² room with a high or raked ceiling.</p>
  <p>For open-plan areas, measure the space the air conditioner will actually need to condition. A lounge that opens directly into a dining area, kitchen and hallway should not automatically be treated as an isolated room.</p>

  <h2>Why there is no simple room-size answer</h2>
  <p>Floor area alone cannot tell you the correct air-conditioner capacity.</p>
  <p>A shaded, insulated bedroom with modest glazing may be relatively easy to cool. The same-sized room can have a much higher cooling load if it is upstairs, receives strong afternoon sun, has large exposed windows or sits beneath a poorly insulated roof.</p>
  <p>That is why generic “X square metres = Y kW” charts should only be treated as rough starting points.</p>
  <p>For a fixed system, use the room measurements to understand the likely range, then have the final capacity checked by the installer.</p>

  <h2>Check sun, windows and orientation</h2>
  <p>Windows can introduce a substantial amount of heat, particularly when exposed to direct summer sun.</p>
  <p>Pay attention to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>north- and west-facing glazing</li>
    <li>large windows and sliding doors</li>
    <li>external awnings, verandahs or other shade</li>
    <li>trees or neighbouring buildings that block sun</li>
    <li>whether the room is upstairs</li>
    <li>how effective the curtains or blinds are.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Orientation should not be considered by itself. A small shaded west-facing window presents a very different heat load from a large unshaded wall of glass facing the same direction.</p>
  <p>Reducing heat gain with effective shading may also improve comfort without simply installing a larger air conditioner.</p>

  <h2>Consider insulation and the roof above</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/home-insulation-basics/">Insulation</a> helps slow heat transfer into the home.</p>
  <p>This is particularly important in rooms directly beneath the roof, where poor ceiling or roof insulation can increase the amount of heat the air conditioner needs to remove.</p>
  <p>If you do not know whether the room is insulated, mention that when requesting quotes. Improving insulation or shading can sometimes be a better long-term solution than asking a larger air conditioner to compensate for excessive heat entering the room.</p>

  <h2>Think about how the room will actually be used</h2>
  <p>Sizing should reflect normal use, not just the dimensions on a floor plan.</p>
  <p>Consider:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>how many people normally use the room</li>
    <li>whether doors to adjoining spaces will stay open</li>
    <li>heat-producing appliances</li>
    <li>large televisions or computers</li>
    <li>cooking areas within an open-plan space</li>
    <li>whether the room needs overnight bedroom cooling.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you intend to cool several connected rooms, tell the installer rather than assuming one system will effectively reach them all.</p>

  <h2>Bigger is not automatically better</h2>
  <p>Buying extra capacity “just to be safe” is not the same as sizing the system correctly.</p>
  <p>An undersized air conditioner may struggle to maintain comfortable conditions during hot weather. An unnecessarily large system can cost more to buy and may operate differently from a correctly sized system, affecting comfort, efficiency and moisture removal.</p>
  <p>The aim is not to buy the biggest unit that fits the budget. It is to match the system to the actual cooling load.</p>

  <h2>Get installation quotes before buying</h2>
  <p>For split systems and other fixed air conditioners, arrange installation quotes before purchasing the unit where possible.</p>
  <p>The installer can assess more than capacity. They can also check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>suitable indoor and outdoor unit locations</li>
    <li>whether a simple back-to-back installation is practical</li>
    <li>refrigerant pipe length</li>
    <li>condensate drainage</li>
    <li>electrical requirements</li>
    <li>access and clearances</li>
    <li>potential noise issues.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Buying a discounted unit first can be false economy if the unit is unsuitable or the proposed installation turns out to be complicated.</p>
  <p>A competent installer may also decline to install customer-supplied equipment they consider unsuitable or unsafe for the proposed installation.</p>

  <h2>Ask whether back-to-back installation is practical</h2>
  <p>A high-wall split system can often be installed with the indoor unit on an external wall and the outdoor unit immediately outside or nearby.</p>
  <p>This usually keeps the refrigerant pipework, drain and cabling relatively short and can reduce installation cost.</p>
  <p>Do not choose a poor condenser location purely to save on pipework, however. The outdoor unit still needs appropriate airflow, clearances, service access and a sensible position in relation to bedrooms and neighbours.</p>
  <p>Our guide to <a href="/guides/split-system-air-conditioner-installation-costs/">split-system installation costs</a> explains how pipe runs, drainage, electrical work, mounting and access can change the quote.</p>

  <h2>Climate matters too</h2>
  <p>Australian air-conditioner efficiency information recognises different climate zones, and local conditions should form part of the purchase decision.</p>
  <p>In humid tropical and subtropical areas, moisture removal can be an important part of comfort as well as temperature reduction. Discuss humidity control, dry mode and wet-season performance when requesting recommendations.</p>

  <h2>Compare models after the sizing decision</h2>
  <p>Once the required capacity has been narrowed down, compare models within that appropriate range.</p>
  <p>Useful factors include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>energy efficiency for your climate</li>
    <li>indoor and outdoor noise</li>
    <li>reverse-cycle heating performance if required</li>
    <li>warranty</li>
    <li>controls and timers</li>
    <li>filter access and maintenance</li>
    <li>dry or dehumidification functions where relevant.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A highly efficient model is still the wrong purchase if it is not appropriately sized for the room. A <a href="/guides/ceiling-fans-before-you-buy/">suitable ceiling fan</a> may also improve air movement without increasing the air conditioner’s capacity.</p>

  <h2>Questions to ask the installer</h2>
  <p>Before accepting a quote, ask:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>What capacity do you recommend, and why?</li>
    <li>What have you allowed for in the room's heat load?</li>
    <li>Is a back-to-back installation suitable?</li>
    <li>Where will the indoor and outdoor units go?</li>
    <li>Is extra electrical work required?</li>
    <li>Where will the condensate drain?</li>
    <li>Are there any noise or access concerns?</li>
    <li>Is there anything about the room that should be improved first?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If two installers recommend very different capacities, ask each to explain the assumptions behind the recommendation.</p>

  <h2>Before you buy</h2>
  <p>Measure the space, look at its sun exposure, glazing and insulation, and think about how the room connects to the rest of the home.</p>
  <p>For a fixed system, get the installation assessed before buying where practical.</p>
  <p>The goal is not to find a universal kilowatt figure for a given floor area. It is to choose a system that suits the room you actually have.</p>

